- How would you validate end-to-end lineage across regions?
- What are failure modes in cross-region DR and mitigations?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a beginner-friendly Azure data pipeline that handles schema drift, produces a dimensional model, and captures lineage. It also probes how to isolate raw data, apply transformations, and enforce simple data quality gates with observable provenance.

## Key Concepts
- Schema drift handling in Parquet
- Dimensional modeling (DimUsers, FactEvents)
- Drift capture via JSON blob
- Data quality checks (NOT NULL, timestamp parse)
- Data lineage with Purview integration

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: drift handling in a dataflow step
function transform(record) {
  const known = { user_id, event_time, event_type };
  const drift = _.omit(record, Object.keys(known));
  return { ...record, drift_json: JSON.stringify(drift) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift handling with evolving schemas?
- How would you extend this to handle very high throughput volumes?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests multi-tenant governance, schema drift handling, lineage, and cost discipline across Azure data services.

## Key Concepts
- Event Hubs ingestion and Spark processing
- Tenant isolation via partitioning and ACLs
- Canonical schema and schema evolution
- Purview for lineage; Delta/Parquet for upserts
- Idempotent loads and per-tenant cost controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-Spark ingestion sketch
const df = spark.read.format("eventhubs").options({
  "eventhubs.connectionString": conn,
  "eventhubs.consumerGroup": "$Default"
}).load();

const parsed = df.selectExpr("CAST(body AS STRING) AS json")
  .select(from_json(col("json"), schema).alias("d")).select("d.*");

parsed.write.partitionBy("tenant_id").mode("append").parquet("<out_path>");
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you ensure schema evolution without breaking downstream?
- How would you monitor data lineage and quality across tenants?
